Experiment 4

Aim:
To install Wireshark, capture network traffic on a specific network interface, and analyze the captured packets to identify different protocols and data types.
Tools Required:
• Wireshark
• Windows OS
• Internet connection
Theory:
Wireshark is a network protocol analyzer used to capture and analyze network packets. It displays detailed transmission information such as the packet source, destination, protocol, packet length, and inner packet details.

Procedure


Part A: Installing Wireshark

1. Open a web browser.
2. Go to the official Wireshark website.
3. Click on the Download option.
4. Download the appropriate Windows installer.
5. Open the downloaded installer file.
6. Follow the setup prompts in the installation wizard and accept the default components.
7. During the installation process, ensure you allow the required Npcap component to be installed.
8. Complete the installation.
9. Launch Wireshark from the Start Menu.
Part B: Capturing Network Traffic

1. Open Wireshark.
2. On the main welcome screen, locate the Capture section.
3. Select the active network interface (such as Wi-Fi or Ethernet).
4. Double-click the active interface (e.g., Wi-Fi) to start capturing packets.
5. Open a web browser and visit a few websites to generate network traffic.
6. Wait for approximately 20–30 seconds.
7. Click the red Stop button in the top toolbar to stop the packet capture.

Part C: Analyzing Captured Packets

The captured packets are displayed in the packet list pane with the following details:
• No.: The sequential packet number.
• Time: The relative time at which the packet was captured.
• Source: The source network address.
• Destination: The destination network address.
• Protocol: The network protocol used by the packet.
• Length: The size of the packet in bytes.
• Info: Additional context and details about the packet contents.

Part D: Applying Display Filters

To isolate and identify specific network protocols, enter the following keywords into the Display Filter box at the top of the screen:
• DNS: dns
• TCP: tcp
• UDP: udp
• ARP: arp
• ICMP: icmp
After entering each filter, press Enter to display only the packets belonging to that specific protocol.

Result

Wireshark was successfully installed and used to capture network traffic. The captured packets were analyzed, and different network protocols along with their packet information were identified successfully.
